sum='Prabhat always wanted to win; he could not bear losing, not even at marbles. If he sensed he would lose, he would not play; he played only games he was sure to win, like badminton. A new student, Surya, an excellent badminton player, joined the school. On the last Friday of every month the coach held friendly matches, and Prabhat and Surya were chosen to play each other; Prabhat was also given the job of keeping score. Prabhat prepared seriously, while Surya was relaxed, smiling and cracking jokes. On Friday Surya was a phenomenon – he won point after point, laughing all the time. But since Surya was paying little attention, Prabhat changed the scoreboard while he looked elsewhere and won by cheating. He boasted, but Surya did not mind: “It’s been fun. We should play again.” That night Prabhat could not sleep – he did not feel happy. Next day he saw Surya playing basketball, so hopeless that he could not score for ten minutes, yet smiling and enjoying. Prabhat watched Surya for some days and realised that to enjoy a game you don’t need a scoreboard or worry about winning or losing – what matters is trying your best and enjoying every moment. Slowly Prabhat changed: he played hide and seek, felt sad when games ended, joked while playing badminton, and others said, “Good fun playing with Prabhat – he has a great sporting spirit.”',
